<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>eu.exeris.tooling</groupId>
        <artifactId>exeris-tooling-root</artifactId>
        <version>0.9.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>exeris-tooling-bom</artifactId>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>

    <name>Exeris Tooling :: BOM</name>
    <description>Bill of materials for tooling modules and third-party deps.</description>

    <properties>
        <!--
          Pinned AHEAD of their releases (B0). The 0.12 line is unpublished on
          both sides, and both pins are built from source: the SDK's `main` is
          `0.12.0-SNAPSHOT`, and the kernel's `development/0.12.0` already
          carries the final string `0.12.0` — code cut 2026-09-03, no `v0.12.0`
          tag yet. A kernel `0.12.0` resolved before that tag is a pre-release
          build that merely looks final; treat it as the SNAPSHOT it is.

          This is a temporary exception to UP0, not a relaxation of it. Both
          pins are pre-release, and both must be final at the cut: no
          cross-repo SNAPSHOT may remain at a tooling cut (ROADMAP "Versioning
          policy"), so both pins move to the final `0.12.0` releases once the
          kernel and the SDK publish, and no tooling release is tagged before
          they do.

          SDK 0.12 needed one source change here, and it is the SDK's record
          policy working as written: a record grows by a trailing component,
          and builders are the stable path. `DomainMetadata` (+ `routeAccess`,
          `channel`) and `ActionMetadata` (+ `routeAccess`) are built through
          their builders, so their growth cost nothing. `SystemFieldsMetadata`
          (+ `sharedScopeField`, the `@SharedScope` carrier) has no builder and
          is built positionally, so it took one trailing argument (Stellar
          finding S6). SDK 0.12's working branch restores the ten-argument
          constructor and adds a builder; neither is on SDK `main` yet. The
          processor fills that argument only for a `DataScope.UNIVERSE` entity,
          from its `@SharedScope` field (T29 slice B), and passes `null`
          everywhere else; the record is `@JsonInclude(NON_NULL)`, so every
          other entity's metadata JSON is unchanged. The `schemaVersion` the
          processor stamps follows `SchemaVersion.CURRENT`, which is "0.12.0" in
          this jar. `@Channel`, `@RouteAccess` and `@SharedScope` are new in
          0.12; `@SharedScope` is extracted (T29 slice B), and the other two by
          nothing yet.
        -->
        <exeris.sdk.version>0.12.0-SNAPSHOT</exeris.sdk.version>
        <!--
          Kernel 0.12.0 — the line SDK 0.12 pairs with. Three of its additions
          are what the emitters can now name, and why this pin moves at all:

          - `FlowDefinitionBuilder.version(int)` (a `default` method, ADR-064
            amendment). The generated `*SagaFlow` can declare `@Saga.version`,
            which it had nowhere to put on 0.11 (T55's emitter half, K5).
          - `ConnectionInterceptor.SESSION_KEY_TENANT_ID` and
            `SESSION_KEY_SHARED_SCOPE`. Both RLS session variables are SPI
            contract now, not one driver's literal, which is what the
            `DataScope.UNIVERSE` transcription was gated on. T29 slice B shipped
            on this pin, and its session-key literal is checked against the
            kernel constant.
          - ADR-074 peer addressing. A CLIENT-mode `HttpConfig`'s `bindHost` /
            `port` are no longer dialled; the peer is `defaultAuthority`
            (`http.client.defaultAuthority`) or `KernelWebClient.withAuthority`.
            No signature moved, so client code that worked on 0.11 compiles
            unchanged and fails at its first request. The emitted `*Client`
            Javadoc says so (K8).

          The ADR-066 baseline still holds, re-measured at B0: the spi, core,
          community and community-testkit jars are all class-file major 69
          with zero preview stamps. (0.10.2 had 9 preview-stamped classes in
          core, which JDK 25 refuses; 0.11.0 was the first clean line.)

          Take the `eu.exeris` coordinates, never `eu.exeris.preview` (the
          JDK 28 EA Valhalla line). Choosing a line is a build-matrix question,
          never a codegen fork: measured at 0.11.0, of the 46 kernel types the
          emitters name only `EventBus` (Javadoc) and `MemoryStats` (the
          `value` modifier) differ, and neither difference reaches a consumer.
        -->
        <exeris.kernel.version>0.12.0</exeris.kernel.version>

        <auto-service.version>1.1.1</auto-service.version>
        <palantir.javapoet.version>0.15.0</palantir.javapoet.version>
        <!-- 2.18.9 closes the jackson-databind advisories Dependabot raises against
             2.18.x. Patch-level, no API change. -->
        <jackson.version>2.18.9</jackson.version>
        <!-- 3.2.2 is the Jackson 3 version kernel 0.12.0 pins (exeris-kernel-bom), and it
             moves with the kernel pin (B0) for the reason this property exists. The comment
             below claims this dep tracks "the same Jackson 3 line the Open-Core kernel
             runtime uses". Management here also beats the kernel's own transitive version:
             left at 3.1.5, the e2e classpath resolved `jackson-databind:3.1.5 - version
             managed from 3.2.2`. GC3 would then have executed kernel 0.12 classes on a
             Jackson one minor older than they were built against, and the compile gate
             would have checked emitted `tools.jackson.*` references against the wrong
             minor.

             Earlier history, same rule: 3.1.5 closed the tools.jackson advisories
             (everything <= 3.1.4 is affected) and ended a drift where this said 3.0.4 while
             kernel 0.10.2 and 0.11.0 pinned 3.1.1. Patch-ahead of the kernel inside its
             minor is a posture for a security bump; a minor behind is not a posture at
             all. -->
        <jackson3.version>3.2.2</jackson3.version>
        <swagger-parser.version>2.1.45</swagger-parser.version>
        <!-- swagger-core carries the OpenAPI model types and the 3.1 mappers this module
             compiles against directly. It arrives transitively through swagger-parser, so
             this pin must track what that version resolves rather than move on its own:
             swagger-parser-project-2.1.45.pom declares <swagger-core-version>2.2.52 at
             line 378, which is where the number below comes from. Declaring it keeps a
             transitive reshuffle from breaking the build silently. -->
        <swagger-core.version>2.2.52</swagger-core.version>

        <junit.version>5.11.4</junit.version>
        <!-- 3.27.7 closes GHSA-rqfh-9r24-8c9r — XXE in isXmlEqualTo when
             parsing untrusted XML. We do not use isXmlEqualTo anywhere
             in the test suite (`grep -r isXmlEqualTo` → zero hits), so
             the vulnerability is structurally non-exploitable here; the
             bump is purely to close the Dependabot alert and keep the
             dep line on a patched version. Patch-level bump (3.27.3 →
             3.27.7), no API change. -->
        <assertj.version>3.27.7</assertj.version>
        <compile-testing.version>0.21.0</compile-testing.version>
        <json-schema-validator.version>1.5.4</json-schema-validator.version>
        <approvaltests.version>24.16.0</approvaltests.version>
        <!-- The JDBC driver the kernel testkit's persistence needs when the e2e suite boots the
             EMITTED Application with the events/flow subsystems (T23 slice B1, T48 slice C1):
             both declare dependsOn("persistence"), and the testkit deliberately ships no driver.
             Same version exeris-kernel-community pins for its own H2 tests (0.12.0 pom), so the
             kernel's persistence has been exercised on exactly this driver. Test scope only —
             emitted code never names it. -->
        <h2.version>2.3.232</h2.version>
    </properties>

    <dependencyManagement>
        <dependencies>
            <!-- Exeris SDK -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>eu.exeris</groupId>
                <artifactId>exeris-sdk-annotations</artifactId>
                <version>${exeris.sdk.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>eu.exeris</groupId>
                <artifactId>exeris-sdk-source-model</artifactId>
                <version>${exeris.sdk.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>eu.exeris</groupId>
                <artifactId>exeris-sdk-composition-spec</artifactId>
                <version>${exeris.sdk.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <!-- SDK boot conductor — not a codegen dependency (the emitter only
                 writes its FQN); needed on the e2e compile gate's test classpath
                 so the GC2 composed Application resolves CompositionConductor. -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>eu.exeris</groupId>
                <artifactId>exeris-sdk-composition-runtime</artifactId>
                <version>${exeris.sdk.version}</version>
            </dependency>

            <!-- Exeris Kernel Open-Core SPI — required by the codegen
                 compile-test to verify emitted code resolves against real
                 SPI types (HttpExchange, HttpStatus, LoanedBuffer, etc.)
                 instead of local stubs. -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>eu.exeris</groupId>
                <artifactId>exeris-kernel-spi</artifactId>
                <version>${exeris.kernel.version}</version>
            </dependency>

            <!-- Exeris Kernel Open-Core CORE — required by the Application
                 emission compile-test for core.bootstrap.KernelBootstrap and
                 core.http.routing.HttpRouter. -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>eu.exeris</groupId>
                <artifactId>exeris-kernel-core</artifactId>
                <version>${exeris.kernel.version}</version>
            </dependency>

            <!-- Community driver + its test fixture — the GC3 composition e2e boots a
                 real kernel (http subsystem only) around the conductor run. -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>eu.exeris</groupId>
                <artifactId>exeris-kernel-community</artifactId>
                <version>${exeris.kernel.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>eu.exeris</groupId>
                <artifactId>exeris-kernel-community-testkit</artifactId>
                <version>${exeris.kernel.version}</version>
            </dependency>

            <!-- Internal tooling modules -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>eu.exeris.tooling</groupId>
                <artifactId>exeris-processor</artifactId>
                <version>${project.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>eu.exeris.tooling</groupId>
                <artifactId>exeris-codegen-core</artifactId>
                <version>${project.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>eu.exeris.tooling</groupId>
                <artifactId>exeris-codegen-java</artifactId>
                <version>${project.version}</version>
            </dependency>

            <!-- Google Auto Service (annotation processor self-registration) -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>com.google.auto.service</groupId>
                <artifactId>auto-service</artifactId>
                <version>${auto-service.version}</version>
            </dependency>

            <!-- JavaPoet (type-safe Java source emission for codegen-java;
                 ADR-015 Amendment 1: Palantir's maintained fork — adds record support
                 for Phase 4c KernelEventGenerator) -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>com.palantir.javapoet</groupId>
                <artifactId>javapoet</artifactId>
                <version>${palantir.javapoet.version}</version>
            </dependency>

            <!-- Jackson 2.x (build-time JSON I/O for metadata files) -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
                <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
                <version>${jackson.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.datatype</groupId>
                <artifactId>jackson-datatype-jsr310</artifactId>
                <version>${jackson.version}</version>
            </dependency>

            <!-- Jackson 3 (tools.jackson) — test-scoped: codegen compile-test
                 verifies emitted repositories' tools.jackson.* references
                 (List<X> JSON-column persistence via ObjectMapper / TypeReference,
                 see KernelRepositoryGenerator) resolve against the same Jackson 3
                 line the Open-Core kernel runtime uses. Handlers no longer
                 reference it — ADR-036 moved request-body decode to the
                 HttpRequestBodyDecoder SPI. Not pulled by production tooling
                 modules. -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>tools.jackson.core</groupId>
                <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
                <version>${jackson3.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.dataformat</groupId>
                <artifactId>jackson-dataformat-yaml</artifactId>
                <version>${jackson.version}</version>
            </dependency>

            <!-- Swagger Parser (OpenAPI 3 emission) -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>io.swagger.parser.v3</groupId>
                <artifactId>swagger-parser</artifactId>
                <version>${swagger-parser.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>io.swagger.core.v3</groupId>
                <artifactId>swagger-core</artifactId>
                <version>${swagger-core.version}</version>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>io.swagger.core.v3</groupId>
                <artifactId>swagger-models</artifactId>
                <version>${swagger-core.version}</version>
            </dependency>

            <!-- Test deps -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
                <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
                <version>${junit.version}</version>
                <scope>test</scope>
            </dependency>
            <!-- Programmatic JUnit execution — the T2 gate does not just compile the
                 generated tests, it RUNS them (a test emitter whose output is never
                 executed is exactly the inert-output failure mode). Platform version
                 tracks the Jupiter one: 5.x.y ↔ platform 1.x.y. -->
            <dependency>
                <groupId>org.junit.platform</groupId>
                <artifactId>junit-platform-launcher</artifactId>
                <version>1.11.4</version>
                <scope>test</scope>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>org.assertj</groupId>
                <artifactId>assertj-core</artifactId>
                <version>${assertj.version}</version>
                <scope>test</scope>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>com.google.testing.compile</groupId>
                <artifactId>compile-testing</artifactId>
                <version>${compile-testing.version}</version>
                <scope>test</scope>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>com.networknt</groupId>
                <artifactId>json-schema-validator</artifactId>
                <version>${json-schema-validator.version}</version>
                <scope>test</scope>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>com.approvaltests</groupId>
                <artifactId>approvaltests</artifactId>
                <version>${approvaltests.version}</version>
                <scope>test</scope>
            </dependency>
            <dependency>
                <groupId>com.h2database</groupId>
                <artifactId>h2</artifactId>
                <version>${h2.version}</version>
                <scope>test</scope>
            </dependency>
        </dependencies>
    </dependencyManagement>
</project>
